= Chronotype — Technical =

'''Chronotype''' concerns stable but modifiable interindividual differences in the timing of daily sleep, wakefulness, preferred activity and associated circadian physiology. In human chronobiology it is often operationalized by morningness–eveningness questionnaire scores or habitual free-day sleep timing. These observables are not identical to one another or to the phase of an endogenous pacemaker. The circadian clock, the sleep homeostatic process, light exposure, work schedules, age and social pressures all influence measured sleep behavior.

Horne and Östberg's 1976 morningness–eveningness questionnaire examined individual preferences and relationships with daily temperature variation. Roenneberg, Wirz-Justice and Merrow's 2003 work developed questionnaires distinguishing temporal behavior on work and free days and helped establish a framework for studying social constraints on sleep timing.<ref name="horne">J. A. Horne and O. Östberg, [https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1027738/ “A Self-Assessment Questionnaire to Determine Morningness-Eveningness in Human Circadian Rhythms”], ''International Journal of Chronobiology'' 4(2), 97–110 (1976).</ref><ref name="roenneberg">Till Roenneberg, Anna Wirz-Justice and Martha Merrow, [https://doi.org/10.1177/0748730402239679 “Life Between Clocks: Daily Temporal Patterns of Human Chronotypes”], ''Journal of Biological Rhythms'' 18(1), 80–90 (2003).</ref>

== What is being measured? ==

A study of chronotype may intend to measure subjective preference, actual sleep behavior, physiological circadian phase or performance at different clock times. The variables overlap, but none can be substituted without validation. Horne and Östberg's original validation included oral temperature measurements from 48 participants; it does not establish that questionnaire scores determine every physiological phase marker.<ref name="horne"/> DLMO is estimated from serial melatonin samples under controlled dim light; report sampling intervals, assay, threshold and recent light exposure because different threshold conventions can yield different phase estimates.<ref name="dlmo">S. Benloucif et al., [https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18350967/ Measuring melatonin in humans], ''Journal of Clinical Sleep Medicine'' 4, 66–69 (2008).</ref>

{| class="wikitable"
! Measurement !! Definition or observable !! Main confound or requirement
|-
| Morningness–Eveningness Questionnaire (MEQ) || Scores from self-reported preferred activity and sleep timing || Self-report, population norms and question interpretation
|-
| Munich ChronoType Questionnaire (MCTQ) || Reported sleep onset and offset on work and free days || Sleep debt, school/work time constraints and recall
|-
